Worth doing

F16, --dry-run: print the resolved command, cwd, env, channel and queue position without queuing anything. It’s small and catches mistakes before a long job is queued.
F14, log auto-clean: delete old logs by age or total size on a timer. It’s small, and it saves you from running clean-logs by hand.
F10, resource accounting: record peak RSS and CPU time per job and show them in qur show. This is useful for sizing DFT and LAMMPS runs, and it’s small to medium.
F12, hooks: --on-finish CMD and --on-fail CMD, for example to rsync results when a job ends. It’s small to medium.
F11, kill signal and grace: qur cancel -s INT --grace 30, so jobs that need a clean shutdown (checkpoint writing) can stop gracefully. It’s small.

Maybe

F13, separate stderr log: keep the merged log and also write a stderr copy. It’s small to medium.
F25, duplicate protection: refuse an identical command and cwd that is already queued unless --force. It’s small.
F17, resource-aware scheduling: delay a job if free RAM or VRAM is below a threshold. It’s medium and needs care, and it’s only worth it if GPU memory clashes actually bite you.
F9, labels and tags: this is the part of F9 still missing. It’s medium.
F15, qur follow / qur top: a live view across jobs. It’s nice but not essential, and it’s medium.
